messages:
customer-dev: Heads up — customer reported being billed twice for the same POST when our API timed out and their client retried. Using ts-sdk@2.4.1 + undici@5.9 on node 18. They say server got the request once, returned 504 after 10s, client retried and server processed second time. Repro below.

maya: thanks — can you paste minimal repro + headers/logs? particularly x-request-id / idempotency if present.

customer-dev: minimal repro (simplified):
```ts
import { RedwoodClient } from '@redwood/inference';
const client = new RedwoodClient({ apiKey: process.env.KEY, retries: 2 });

async function call() {
  const ac = new AbortController();
  setTimeout(() => ac.abort(), 8000); // app-level timeout shorter than server processing
  try {
    await client.post('/v1/process', { body: JSON.stringify({ user: 123 }), signal: ac.signal });
  } catch (e) {
    // our wrapper retries on network errors
    // naive retry here:
    await client.post('/v1/process', { body: JSON.stringify({ user: 123 }) });
  }
}
call();
```

Logs from server show two separate POSTs within ~12s. No idempotency key used.

sam: a couple of things to check: 1) ts-sdk has its own retry/backoff for 5xx/network errors. If your app also retries on top of SDK retries you can get duplicate attempts. 2) undici + AbortController interaction can cause the SDK to treat the aborted request as a network error and immediately retry while the backend may still be processing the original.

kevin-eng: we saw a similar pattern in an internal case — when the client aborts via AbortController the TCP connection can still allow the server to finish processing. The SDK's retry logic then issues a second POST. If requests are not idempotent you get double side-effects / billing.

customer-dev: how should they mitigate quickly in prod?

maya: short-term mitigation options:
- Add an idempotency key header on POSTs (recommended for any non-idempotent operation).
- Disable client-side retry wrapper or set sdk retries to 0 while you investigate. Example: new RedwoodClient({ retries: 0 }) or pass `retries: 0` per call.
- Increase the app AbortController timeout to exceed server processing time (not ideal).

sam: medium-term: upgrade SDK. We have a fix in v2.5.0 that better coordinates AbortController handoff and avoids immediate retry when signal.abort was the cause (internal ticket RGW-4827). It also exposes a way to set an idempotency header automatically for POSTs when you provide an idempotencyKey option.

kevin-eng: repro steps for engineering to reproduce locally:
1) Start a simple express endpoint that sleeps 9s then returns 200.
2) Use undici in node + ts-sdk to POST with an AbortController that aborts at 8s.
3) Observe server logs: first request completes, client sees abort/network error and triggers SDK retry + app retry.

example server snippet:
```js
const express = require('express');
const app = express();
app.use(express.json());
app.post('/v1/process', async (req, res) => {
  console.log('received', req.headers['x-id'] || '-');
  await new Promise(r => setTimeout(r, 9000));
  res.json({ ok: true });
});
app.listen(3000);
```

ops-bot: attached ticket RGW-4827 created and assigned to eng-runtime.

customer-dev: ok we'll patch by adding idempotency keys and temporarily set retries:0. We'll test upgrade path to 2.5.0 once it's out. Thanks.

maya: follow-up: please DM the customer asking for request/response traces (x-request-id, sdk-request-id) if available — those are handy for confirmation. Also note: billing team can refund duplicate charge once verified.

sam: also added a short KB note for support: "TS SDK + AbortController: avoid app-layer retry + enable idempotency for POSTs".

kevin-eng: marking as triaged, will push the AbortController-handshake fix in next patch and add docs on idempotency usage.

customer-dev: closing here, will reopen if duplicates persist.
